00:10:46,600 --> 00:11:02,060
Let's see here.

271
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:05,400
Okay. So, anyway, popular simplifying assumption is

272
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,920
that the only things that people care about are the mean

273
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:11,880
of their consumption and the variance of their consumption.

274
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:14,440
All right.

275
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:17,360
And, of course, people want to have what kind

276
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:18,940
of mean is better?

277
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:21,840
Higher. And what kind of variance is better?

278
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:23,240
Lower. All right.

279
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:26,520
Yes. So, and that's a simple function that you can use

280
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:29,200
in order to define what people's preferences are.

281
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:32,040
So, of course, if you think about it, this,

282
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:36,280
if you start actually putting numbers on it,

283
00:11:36,280 --> 00:11:37,420
it could apply to some odd results,

284
00:11:37,420 --> 00:11:40,060
but anyway, commonly used just for simplicity.

285
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:42,580
Right? And this gives rise to the idea

286
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:43,780
of mean variance efficiency.

287
00:11:43,780 --> 00:11:46,140
Which is a lot like any other kind

288
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:47,580
of utility optimization problem.

289
00:11:48,400 --> 00:11:51,620
So, assumes that there's going to be some kind

290
00:11:51,620 --> 00:11:54,320
of a mean variance frontier.

291
00:11:55,280 --> 00:11:57,560
Right? So, there is a, you know,

292
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,120
so for any given variance there's a maximum mean you can

293
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:02,780
get. For any given mean there is a minimum variance

294
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:03,360
that you can get.

295
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:04,920
And people are trying to get

296
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,920
on to the frontier of those combinations.

297
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:09,820
Right? So, this is easiest to think

298
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:12,060
about if you actually reverse the scales.

299
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:15,460
So, like here we have mean and that means bigger.

300
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:21,960
And then here we have variance and that means smaller.

301
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:23,880
Yes. Yes.

302
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:24,580
Yeah. Yeah.

303
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:26,280
It could be one over variance and bigger.

304
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:29,340
Right? And then we'll have something like that.

305
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:31,180
So, like our usual kind of budget constraint

306
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:34,260
where we'll say yes, yes, you could go

307
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:35,560
and get a smaller variance, but then you're going to have

308
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:36,480
to settle for a lower mean.

309
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:37,940
Or you could get a higher variance

310
00:12:37,940 --> 00:12:40,020
and then have a lower mean.

311
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:42,020
We're going to assume you have higher variance, higher mean,

312
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:43,020
lower variance, lower mean.

313
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:45,420
Okay? All right.

314
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:47,780
So, anyway, and that's the mean variance.

315
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:49,120
So, as long as you're on that frontier,

316
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:50,460
then you've got mean variance efficiency.

317
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:52,100
Right? Does that make sense?

318
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:54,740
So, use the general idea of being on a production frontier

319
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:55,680
or consumption frontier.

320
00:12:56,460 --> 00:12:56,980
Same idea.

321
00:12:57,580 --> 00:12:59,420
Okay? Okay.

322
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:02,080
Now, if you work through all these assumptions,

323
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:02,880
which I'm not going to do.

324
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:04,040
I'm just going to tell you the answer.

325
00:13:04,520 --> 00:13:06,820
So, we work through the assumptions of a model

326
00:13:06,820 --> 00:13:08,780
where people only care about the mean and variance.

327
00:13:08,780 --> 00:13:11,680
Then you wind up getting the following equation

328
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:13,140
for the expected return on asset.

329
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:17,140
And it's a bunch of symbols, which I know most

330
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:18,280
of which will be unfamiliar to you.

331
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:20,680
But I'll write them down and then explain each part

332
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:21,280
of the equation.

333
00:13:21,280 --> 00:13:28,080
So, we have R A bar equals R naught plus sigma A E

334
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:34,320
over sigma E E all of this times R E bar minus R naught.

335
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:35,820
All right.

336
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:36,740
So, what's the translation?

337
00:13:36,740 --> 00:13:41,020
So, this is the expected return on asset A. So,

338
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,700
that's the expected return on asset A. And so,

339
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:45,920
R sub A is the expected return on asset A.

340
00:13:46,420 --> 00:13:48,220
R naught is the risk-free rate,

341
00:13:48,220 --> 00:13:52,920
which we usually operationalize as thresholds in the States.

342
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:53,420
All right?

343
00:13:54,420 --> 00:14:00,140
And then this is plus, plus the ratio of the covariance

344
00:14:00,140 --> 00:14:04,300
of A's return to the variance of the average market return.

345
00:14:04,300 --> 00:14:08,980
So, E is the market basket.

346
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:10,840
A is this particular asset.

347
00:14:11,280 --> 00:14:13,500
So, remember this here is the covariance between A and E

348
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:16,380
over the variance between E, variance of E.

349
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:20,160
And remember what is covariance of A with E

350
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:23,140
over the variance of E?

351
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:27,140
Remember there's, you remember covariance

352
00:14:27,140 --> 00:14:29,060
over variance of that equation?

353
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:31,320
Yes, so that's correlation coefficient.

354
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:33,280
Yes, correlation coefficient.

355
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:36,360
Right? So, that's the correlation coefficient times

356
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:40,620
the difference between the average market rate.

357
00:14:40,980 --> 00:14:42,540
So, this is not the risk-free rate.

358
00:14:42,540 --> 00:14:44,220
This is the rate that you get

359
00:14:44,220 --> 00:14:45,960
from holding the market portfolio on average.

360
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:47,920
And then that's the risk-free rate.

361
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:49,960
So, the difference between,

362
00:14:50,260 --> 00:14:52,600
from holding the well-diversified market

363
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:55,540
on market bundle, which you think of as say the S&P 500

364
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:56,280
or something like that.

365
00:14:56,740 --> 00:14:58,320
And then that would be the treasury bill rate.

366
00:14:59,560 --> 00:15:02,600
Okay. And again, so this is actually,

367
00:15:02,600 --> 00:15:05,320
you know, the coefficient you would get if you regressed,

368
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:08,240
if you were to go and regress asset A's return

369
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:09,540
onto the average market return.

370
00:15:10,360 --> 00:15:12,560
And remember, and this is also called the beta.

371
00:15:12,560 --> 00:15:14,180
If you ever wondered why is it called the beta,

372
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:16,340
remember how we called regressions coefficients beta,

373
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:17,520
regression coefficients betas?

374
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:18,620
Remember that?

375
00:15:19,100 --> 00:15:21,760
And then people talk about the beta of an asset.

376
00:15:22,380 --> 00:15:23,620
Well, it's not a coincidence.

377
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:25,420
Right? Basically, imagine, you know,

378
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:29,700
you're essentially regressing the price of the one asset

379
00:15:29,700 --> 00:15:32,860
on the overall price of the market.

380
00:15:33,320 --> 00:15:34,620
And then you get this correlation.

381
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:36,020
And then once you do that,

382
00:15:36,300 --> 00:15:38,380
you're going to just say it's regressing the return.

383
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:42,720
So, regress the return of say, I don't know,

384
00:15:43,060 --> 00:15:44,860
Dell on the overall market.

385
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:46,560
And that will give you the beta of Dell,

386
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:47,940
which is just the regression coefficient

387
00:15:47,940 --> 00:15:50,480
for regressing Dell onto market return.

388
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:53,420
Okay. Yes?

389
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:55,160
This is the capital asset pricing.

390
00:15:55,160 --> 00:15:55,780
Yeah, exactly.

391
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:57,240
This is also called CAPM.

392
00:15:57,360 --> 00:15:58,680
Yes, capital asset pricing model.

393
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:01,580
So, you know, it's the basic one.

394
00:16:01,580 --> 00:16:02,740
There's many variations.

395
00:16:02,820 --> 00:16:05,540
But this is where people usually start.

